\title[No-Decoupling for Quadratic-Form Sieving]{No-Decoupling for Quadratic-Form Sieving:\\ Column Space, Split Set, and the Complete-Cost Rule}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
In the quadratic sieve and its multi-polynomial variants, the linear algebra over $\F_2$ has one column for every prime that can divide a sieved value. It is classical that for $x^2-N$ only primes at which $N$ is a quadratic residue occur. We ask whether any of the three standard per-instance enhancements---the large-prime variation, Silverman's multiple polynomials, and adaptive choice of the smoothness bound $B$---can move the column space off this \emph{split set}. We show that none can, and that all three cases follow from the identity $4A\,f(x)=(2Ax+B)^2-D$ for a form $f(x)=Ax^2+Bx+C$ of discriminant $D$. Every prime divisor of every value of $f$ makes $D$ a square, and for odd $p\nmid A$ the column set of $f$ equals the split set of $D$. A Silverman family has one common column set (not the ``quarter'' obtained from two unrelated discriminants); the large prime of a partial relation is itself split, and a large-prime pair has odd-exponent support inside the small split set; an adaptive bound only reparametrises the ray $Q\approx m/2$. The one surviving lever---extra polynomials buy rows at zero marginal column cost, so $|\SB(D)|+1$ smooth values always contain a square regardless of the number of forms---is gated by $B^2M\lesssim\sqrt{N/2}$, derived from the exact minimax $\min_A\max(N/A,\,AM^2-N/A)=M\sqrt{N/2}$; the gate fails by a factor exceeding $600$ for all $N<2^{42}$ with $B,M\ge1000$. Finally we prove that the elasticity of a total cost $S+L$ is the cost-share-weighted average of the component elasticities, positive iff $L'>-S'$, explaining an observed sign inversion between sieve-only and complete-cost pricing. Two earlier claims in this line of work are corrected, and a cubic example marks the boundary of the theory.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The quadratic sieve (QS) factors an odd composite $N$ by producing many congruences $X_i^2\equiv V_i\pmod N$ with $V_i$ $B$-smooth, and then finding a subset whose product of $V_i$ is a perfect square. Writing each smooth $V_i$ as an exponent vector modulo $2$ turns this into finding a dependency among vectors in $\F_2^{Q}$, where $Q$ is the number of primes that can actually occur---the \emph{columns}. Each smooth value is a \emph{row}. Once the number of rows exceeds $Q$, a dependency exists, and it yields a nontrivial factor via $\gcd(X-Y,N)$ with probability at least about $1/2$.

For $x^2-N$, a prime $p$ dividing a value satisfies $x^2\equiv N\pmod p$, so only primes with $\leg Np\in\{0,1\}$ appear. We call this set the \emph{split set}; it contains roughly half of the primes up to $B$.

Practical sieves add three enhancements: \emph{large primes} (retain values smooth apart from one prime $r\in(B,L]$ and combine pairs sharing $r$); \emph{multiple polynomials} (sieve a family of forms $f_t(x)=A_tx^2+B_tx+C_t$ over short intervals $[-M,M]$); and \emph{adaptive $B$}. Can any of these \emph{decouple} the column space from the split set?

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Main conclusions.}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item No-decoupling is a theorem for all three channels (Sections~\ref{sec:col}--\ref{sec:lp}): any form whose discriminant is tied to $N$ forces the split set, so column space and split set are the same object.
\item The full cost inverts the sign (Section~\ref{sec:cost}): pricing only the sieve makes larger $Q$ look favourable; pricing sieve plus $\F_2$ elimination makes it unfavourable.
\item One lever survives, on the row side (Section~\ref{sec:row}): the number of polynomials is orthogonal to the number of columns, within a regime limited by an explicit gate that fails by nearly three orders of magnitude at 41--42 bits.
\item Boundary (Section~\ref{sec:cubic}): outside differences of squares, non-split columns appear.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

An integer $D$ is a \emph{square mod $p$} if $r^2\equiv D\pmod p$ for some $r$ (including $D\equiv0$).

\begin{definition}[Quadratic form]
A \emph{quadratic form} is an integer polynomial $f(x)=Ax^2+Bx+C$, with \emph{discriminant} $\disc f=B^2-4AC$. The basic QS polynomial $x^2-N$ has discriminant $4N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Factor base, split set, column set]
For a bound $B$, the \emph{factor base} is the set of primes $p\le B$. For $D\in\Z$, the \emph{split set} is
$\SB(D)=\{p\le B\text{ prime}: D\text{ is a square mod }p\}$.
The \emph{column set} of a form $f$ is the set of primes $p\le B$ dividing at least one value $f(x)$; for a family it is the union over its members.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Silverman family]
For a prime $q$ with $\leg Nq=1$, set $A=q^2$, choose $b$ with $b^2\equiv N\pmod{q^2}$, and let $c=(b^2-N)/A$. The form $f(x)=Ax^2+2bx+c$ satisfies $\disc f=4N$ and $A f(x)=(Ax+b)^2-N$. A family of such forms is a \emph{Silverman family}; all members share the discriminant $4N$ (or $kN$, $4kN$ with a multiplier $k$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partial relation, LP-pair]
A \emph{partial relation} is $x^2-N=s\cdot r$ with $s$ $B$-smooth and $r$ prime. Two partial relations sharing $r$ form an \emph{LP-pair}, combined into $(s_1r)(s_2r)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Elasticity]
For a positive differentiable cost $C(u)$ with $u=\log Q$, its \emph{elasticity} with respect to $Q$ is $\frac{d}{du}\log C=C'(u)/C(u)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The discriminant identity and the Column Theorem}\label{sec:col}

\begin{lemma}[Discriminant identity]\label{lem:disc}
For every form $f=(A,B,C)$ and every $x\in\Z$, \ $4A\,f(x)=(2Ax+B)^2-\disc f$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(2Ax+B)^2-(B^2-4AC)=4A^2x^2+4ABx+4AC=4A\,f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:qc}
If a prime $p$ divides $y^2-D$, then $D$ is a square mod $p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Columns force the split set]\label{thm:force}
For any form $f$ and any prime $p$ (including $p=2$ and $p\mid A$): if $p\mid f(x)$ for some $x$, then $\disc f$ is a square mod $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$p\mid 4Af(x)=(2Ax+B)^2-\disc f$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:qc} with $y=2Ax+B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Column Theorem]\label{thm:col}
Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p\nmid A$. Then $p$ divides some value $f(x)$ if and only if $\disc f$ is a square mod $p$. Hence, away from primes dividing $2A$, the column set of $f$ equals $\SB(\disc f)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
One direction is Theorem~\ref{thm:force}. Conversely let $r^2\equiv\disc f\pmod p$. As $2A$ is invertible mod $p$, choose $x\equiv(r-B)(2A)^{-1}$. Then $2Ax+B\equiv r$, so $4Af(x)\equiv r^2-\disc f\equiv0$, and since $4A$ is a unit, $p\mid f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:force} has no hypotheses: no prime outside the split set of the discriminant ever appears. Theorem~\ref{thm:col} shows that the inclusion is an equality at all but finitely many primes.
\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}[Square-class invariance]\label{lem:sq}
If $p\nmid s$, then $Ds^2$ is a square mod $p$ iff $D$ is. In particular $kN$ and $4kN$ have the same split set at odd primes.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $D\equiv r^2$ then $Ds^2\equiv(rs)^2$; if $Ds^2\equiv r^2$ then $D\equiv(rs^{-1})^2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Multiple polynomials: a shared discriminant is one constraint}\label{sec:mp}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-polynomial support]\label{thm:support}
Let $(f_t)_{t\in\mathcal T}$ be any family with $\disc f_t=D$ for all $t$. If a prime $p\le B$ divides some $f_t(x)$, then $p\in\SB(D)$. The column set of the whole family lies in the single split set $\SB(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:force} applied to $f_t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Identical column sets]\label{thm:same}
If $\disc f=\disc g$, then at every odd prime dividing neither leading coefficient, $p$ divides some value of $f$ iff it divides some value of $g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:col} both conditions are equivalent to ``$\disc f=\disc g$ is a square mod $p$''.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A conjunction of identical constraints equals one]\label{thm:conj}
For a nonempty family with common discriminant $D$, the set of primes $p\le B$ at which every $\disc f_t$ is a square mod $p$ equals $\SB(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every condition in the conjunction is the single condition ``$D$ is a square mod $p$''.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Silverman's square relation]
If $A=a^2$ then $(2a)^2f(x)\equiv(2Ax+B)^2\pmod{\disc f}$ for all $x$. Thus each value of a Silverman form is, up to a square factor, a square modulo $N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:disc} with $4A=(2a)^2$, the difference $(2a)^2f(x)-(2Ax+B)^2$ equals $-\disc f$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Contrast with unrelated discriminants.} If $D_1D_2$ is not a square, the four sign patterns of $\big(\leg{D_1}p,\leg{D_2}p\big)$ are asymptotically equidistributed, so primes at which both are squares have density $1/4$. That phenomenon belongs to a different construction, not to Silverman's. Empirically, on 41--42-bit $N$, the multi-polynomial column set occupied $0.487$--$0.505$ of $\pi(B)$, identical to single-polynomial QS, whereas unrelated pairs gave $0.244$--$0.317$.

\section{Large primes and adaptive $B$}\label{sec:lp}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]
If $B\le L$ then $\SB(N)\subseteq\mathcal S_L(N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The large prime is split]
If $x^2-N=s\cdot r$ with $r$ prime of any size, then $r\in\mathcal S_L(N)$ for every $L\ge r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$r\mid x^2-N$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:qc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[LP-pair support]\label{thm:lp}
Let $x_1^2-N=s_1r$, $x_2^2-N=s_2r$ with $r$ prime and $s_1,s_2$ nonzero and $B$-smooth. Every prime occurring to an odd power in $(s_1r)(s_2r)$ lies in the small split set $\SB(N)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$v_p\big((s_1r)(s_2r)\big)=v_p(s_1)+v_p(s_2)+2v_p(r)$. If odd, then $p\mid s_1$ or $p\mid s_2$, say $p\mid s_1$; then $p\le B$ and $p\mid x_1^2-N$, so $p\in\SB(N)$.
\end{proof}

Large primes therefore enlarge the row supply without enlarging the effective column space: the large-prime variation is a monotonicity identity, not a discovery, which is why every production QS includes it. Empirically the number of large-prime columns equalled the split-set prediction on $68/68$ instances.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Adaptive $B$.} With $m=\pi(B)$ and $Q=|\SB(N)|\sim m/2$, changing $B$ moves $(m,Q)$ along the ray $Q\approx m/2$. The measured slope was $d\log Q/d\log m=0.99718\pm0.03722$: the bound is a reparametrisation, not a second degree of freedom.

\section{The row-side lever}\label{sec:row}

\begin{theorem}[$\F_2$ dependency]\label{thm:f2}
Let $\mathcal S$ be a finite set of primes and $V_1,\dots,V_n$ nonzero integers whose prime factors lie in $\mathcal S$. If $n>|\mathcal S|$, some nonempty sub-product of the $V_i$ is a perfect square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The $n$ parity vectors in $\F_2^{|\mathcal S|}$ are linearly dependent; a dependency is a square product.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-polynomial congruence of squares]\label{thm:mpcs}
Let $(f_t)_{t\in\mathcal T}$ be any family, of any size, with common discriminant $D$. Given $n$ pairs $(t_i,x_i)$ with $V_i=|f_{t_i}(x_i)|$ nonzero and $B$-smooth, if $n>|\SB(D)|$ then some nonempty sub-product of the $V_i$ is a perfect square. The threshold does not depend on $|\mathcal T|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each prime factor of $V_i$ lies in $\SB(D)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:support}; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:f2}.
\end{proof}

The $T$-th polynomial thus buys rows at zero marginal rank cost (a sign column is added in practice). This is a row-side effect, not a decoupling of the column space.

\begin{theorem}[Silverman minimax]\label{thm:minimax}
For all $N,M,A>0$, \ $\max\big(N/A,\ AM^2-N/A\big)\ge M\sqrt{N/2}$, with equality at $A^\ast=\sqrt{2N}/M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $m=M\sqrt{N/2}$ and $y=N/A$. If $y\ge m$ we are done. Otherwise $AM^2=2m^2/y$ and $2m^2/y-y\ge m$ is equivalent to $(m-y)(2m+y)\ge0$. At $A^\ast$, $N/A^\ast=M\sqrt{N/2}$ and $A^\ast M^2-N/A^\ast=2M\sqrt{N/2}-M\sqrt{N/2}$.
\end{proof}

Since Silverman's leading coefficients are $q^2$ with $q>B$, one has $A>B^2$; for forms to sit near $A^\ast$ one needs $B^2M\lesssim\sqrt{2N}$. We state the gate in the normalisation of the minimax value, $B^2M\lesssim\sqrt{N/2}\approx0.707\sqrt N$; the constant is immaterial in view of:

\begin{theorem}[The gate fails at 42 bits]
If $N<2^{42}$ and $B,M\ge1000$, then $B^2M>600\sqrt{N/2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sqrt{N/2}<\sqrt{2^{41}}<1.49\times10^6<10^9/600\le B^2M/600$.
\end{proof}

This explains the observed saturation of the multi-polynomial gain at $T\approx4$: the ratio $A_{\mathrm{actual}}/A_{\mathrm{ideal}}$ climbed from $13.8$ to $1477$ as $T$ grew.

\section{The complete-cost rule}\label{sec:cost}

Let $u=\log Q$, $S(u)>0$ the sieve cost and $L(u)>0$ the $\F_2$ elimination cost, with derivatives $s',l'$.

\begin{theorem}[Elasticity of a total cost]
$\displaystyle \frac{d}{du}\log(S+L)=\frac{S}{S+L}\cdot\frac{s'}{S}+\frac{L}{S+L}\cdot\frac{l'}{L}=\frac{s'+l'}{S+L}.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Chain rule.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Betweenness]
The total elasticity lies between $\min(s'/S,l'/L)$ and $\max(s'/S,l'/L)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sign criterion]\label{cor:sign}
The total elasticity is positive iff $l'>-s'$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sieve-only pricing inverts the sign]
If $s'<0$ and $l'>-s'$, the sieve-only elasticity is negative while the complete-cost elasticity is positive.
\end{corollary}

\noindent\textbf{Empirical instance.} On 80 instances, $d\log S/d\log Q=-0.1490\pm0.1299$ (negative on $73/80$) while $d\log(S+L)/d\log Q=+0.0787\pm0.1472$ (positive on $60/80$, $t\approx+4.8$). The elimination term grew by $146\times$ to $3981\times$ while the sieve term was flat above $B=2400$.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Rule.} \emph{A per-instance lever on QS cost must be priced against the complete cost of reaching a factor---sieve work plus the $\F_2$ elimination that grows with $Q$. Any reported $d\log(\mathrm{cost})/d\log Q$ must state which terms are in the cost.}

\section{The boundary: leaving differences of squares}\label{sec:cubic}

\begin{proposition}
There exist $N,x,p$ with $p\mid x^3-N$ but $N$ not a square mod $p$: take $N=2$, $x=3$, $p=5$, since $3^3-2=25$ and the squares mod $5$ are $\{0,1,4\}$.
\end{proposition}

For $N=1{,}099{,}515{,}822{,}059=1048573\cdot1048583$, eleven non-split primes below $200$ divide values $x^3-N$ for $x$ in a window of length $2000$. This is the only route to non-split columns, and it leads out of QS into the number field sieve direction.

\section{Corrections to earlier claims}

\noindent\textbf{Multi-polynomial column fraction.} An earlier claim of density $\approx1/4$ is wrong: Silverman forms share the discriminant $kN$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:conj} the density is $\approx1/2$ (measured $0.487$--$0.505$). The value $1/4$ belongs to two unrelated discriminants (measured $0.244$--$0.317$). The no-decoupling conclusion stands and is Theorem~\ref{thm:support}.

\noindent\textbf{Adaptive $B$ direction.} A claimed favourable direction, interior optimum at $B=9600$, and oracle saving of $0.9533\times$ rested on sieve-only pricing and are withdrawn; under complete-cost pricing the sign reverses. The reparametrisation result stands.

\noindent\textbf{Data audit.} For the large-prime experiment the committed summary had been written before its data and misreported the comparison (the true counts were 54--64 of 66 in favour of the arm declared the loser). For the adaptive-$B$ experiment, quoted cost figures appeared in no record; the true flat-basin geometric means were $3835.5$, $3833.0$, $3795.4$, $3811.9$, $3831.1$. A permutation null ($20000$ resamples) confirmed the effect magnitude $0.0478$ is not noise, but it is unreachable by an instance-level lever.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Split set]
For each prime $p\le B$: accept if $p=2$ or $p\mid D$, otherwise iff $D^{(p-1)/2}\equiv1\pmod p$. Cost $O(\pi(B)\log B)$ modular multiplications. Sieve roots are $x\equiv(\pm\sqrt D-B)(2A)^{-1}\pmod p$ via Tonelli--Shanks.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Silverman family]
For successive primes $q>B$ near $(\sqrt{2N}/M)^{1/2}$ with $\leg Nq=1$: compute $r=\sqrt N\bmod q$, lift $b\equiv r+q\big((N-r^2)/q\big)(2r)^{-1}\pmod{q^2}$, set $A=q^2$, $c=(b^2-N)/A$, emit $(A,2b,c)$. All forms have discriminant $4N$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Square from $|\SB(D)|+1$ rows]
Gaussian elimination over $\F_2$ on parity vectors, tracking combinations; the first vector reducing to zero gives the dependency. Dense cost $O(Q^3)$ bit operations; this growth in $Q$ is the term $L$ of Section~\ref{sec:cost}.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[LP pairing]
Bucket partial relations by large prime; combine within buckets. By Theorem~\ref{thm:lp} each combination is a full relation over $\SB(N)$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Numerical illustration}

For $N=1{,}099{,}515{,}822{,}059$: with $B=400$ ($\pi(B)=78$) the primes dividing some $x^2-N$ and the split set coincide ($41$ primes, fraction $0.526$). Four Silverman forms with $A\in\{419^2,431^2,433^2,449^2\}$ all have discriminant $4N$ and the same $40$ odd columns. Among $1368$ partial relations with large prime in $(300,15000]$ none was non-split, and all $344$ LP-pairs had odd support in the small split set. With $B=120$ ($|\SB|=15$) and $M=20000$, one, two, four and eight forms gave $4$, $6$, $13$, $33$ smooth rows, never touching more than $15$ columns; the first $16$ rows contained a square product.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The three channels differ operationally but each produces values $y^2-D$ with $D$ tied to $N$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:disc} converts every prime divisor into a square root of $D$. All freedom in multi-polynomial sieving is on the row side; the scarce resource is admissible leading coefficients near the minimax optimum. The complete-cost identity applies to any multi-stage pipeline.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \emph{Norm-form column theorem for degree-$d$ sieves:} for $f$ monic irreducible of degree $d$ and $p\nmid\disc f$, $p$ divides some $f(x)$ iff $f$ has a root mod $p$; the column density is the Chebotarev proportion of Galois elements with a fixed point, equal to $1/2$ only for $d=2$.
\item \emph{Complete-cost elasticity sign theorem:} with $S(B)\sim\pi(B)u^u$ and $L(B)\sim\pi(B)^\omega$, $\omega\in[2,3]$, show the total elasticity is positive above the sieve-optimal bound by a factor $(\log N)^c$.
\item \emph{Row-side saturation law:} the multi-polynomial gain saturates once $T$ exceeds the number of primes in $\big(B,(\sqrt{2N}/M)^{1/2}\big]$.
\item \emph{Large-prime graph cycle rank:} full relations from partials equal the cycle rank $E-V+C$ of the large-prime graph, each with odd support in the $B$-split set.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For quadratic-form sieving the decoupling question is closed: large primes, multiple polynomials and adaptive bounds cannot move the column space off the split set. One row-side lever survives, gated by $B^2M\lesssim\sqrt{N/2}$, which fails by more than $600\times$ at 42 bits. And the cost of any lever must be computed on the complete pipeline, since the elasticity of a sum is the share-weighted average of its parts.

